/* Track: Experiments in production. Ordered foundational first. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "experiments-instrument-earns-trust",
  track: "experiments", level: "core",
  title: "An online controlled experiment is a measurement instrument that has to earn trust",
  source: "Ron Kohavi, Diane Tang and Ya Xu, Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments: A Practical Guide to A/B Testing, 2020",
  idea: "The randomisation gives you the causal claim, but only the plumbing and the checks make the number worth acting on.",
  why: "The first word of the title is doing the work. Randomisation buys you one thing: the two arms differ only by the treatment and by chance. Everything else that stands between the assignment and the number on the dashboard - the flag evaluation, the event emission, the client that drops events on a slow network, the bot filter, the join key, the aggregation window - is ordinary software, and ordinary software is wrong at ordinary rates. A t-test cannot tell you that one arm lost four percent of its telemetry.\n\nSo the failure distribution is lopsided. A statistically sophisticated analysis of a corrupt pipeline is confidently wrong, and confidently wrong is worse than no measurement, because a team that has no number stays cautious while a team that has a bad number ships. Most of the book is checks: sample ratio, A/A behaviour, guardrails, outliers, segment consistency, novelty decay. The statistics chapter is short because the statistics is the easy part.",
  failureMode: "A feature shows a three percent lift in task completion. Three weeks later someone notices the treatment arm added a client-side event that fires on render while the control arm fires on commit. The lift was the instrumentation. The feature had already shipped, and the three percent is now in a board deck.",
  experiment: "Take one number you have used to justify a decision in the last month - a Grafana panel, a ClickHouse aggregate, anything. Write the four-step chain that produced it: who was assigned, what event was emitted, what was filtered out, how it was aggregated. For each step, write down whether a bug there would show up as an obvious break or as a plausible number. Count the steps in the second category. That count is your current exposure.",
  reflection: "Which step in the chain could be wrong right now without anyone noticing, and what would the cheapest automated check on it look like?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is an untrustworthy experiment described as worse than running no experiment at all?",
    a: "Because it converts uncertainty into false confidence. Without a number a team hedges, stages the rollout and watches; with a number that looks authoritative it commits.\n\nThe asymmetry is that the failure modes of the pipeline produce plausible results rather than obviously broken ones, so nothing in the output signals that the instrument was faulty.",
  },
  deepDive: "Here is how one of my agent metrics is computed end to end; walk the chain and tell me at which step a silent bug would produce a plausible but wrong number."
},
{
  id: "experiments-srm",
  track: "experiments", level: "applied",
  title: "Sample ratio mismatch invalidates the result before you read it",
  source: "Aleksander Fabijan, Jayant Gupchup, Somit Gupta, Jeff Omhover, Wen Qin, Lukas Vermeer and Pavel Dmitriev, Diagnosing Sample Ratio Mismatch in Online Controlled Experiments, KDD, 2019",
  idea: "If the observed split between arms does not match the designed split, stop analysing and start debugging.",
  why: "The test is one line. For a designed 50/50 split, chi-squared is the sum over arms of (observed minus expected) squared divided by expected, with one degree of freedom. With 50,000 expected per arm and an observed 50,600 versus 49,400, that is 600 squared over 50,000, doubled, which is 14.4, and the p-value is about 0.00015. Fabijan and colleagues use a threshold of 0.0005 rather than 0.05, because the check runs on every experiment and you want alarms you will actually investigate.\n\nWhy it matters more than any other single check: the mechanisms that lose users are almost never random with respect to the outcome. Redirect bugs lose slow connections. Bot filters applied after assignment strip different populations from each arm. Telemetry that fails under load drops the users having the worst experience. So the missing users are disproportionately the ones whose behaviour would have moved the metric, and the surviving comparison is between two populations that are no longer exchangeable. Randomisation has been undone downstream, and no amount of variance reduction repairs it.",
  failureMode: "An experiment reports a strong win on engagement. The split is 50.4 percent to 49.6 percent, which looks like nothing until you compute it on two million users and get a p-value near zero. The treatment arm was redirecting through an extra hop that timed out for users on poor networks, and those users were exactly the low-engagement tail. The win is the missing tail.",
  experiment: "Find any binary routing decision in your system that should be balanced - a feature flag, a hash-based shard, a canary weight - and count distinct units per branch in ClickHouse for the last seven days. Compute the chi-squared statistic by hand in a spreadsheet against the designed ratio and get a p-value. If you have no live split, use synthetic counts: check that 50,200 against 49,800 gives 1.6 and passes, and 50,600 against 49,400 gives 14.4 and fails. You want the arithmetic in your fingers before you need it.",
  reflection: "What is the designed ratio for the next thing you roll out, and where will the count of units per arm be queryable from?",
  recall: {
    q: "You get a p-value of 0.0001 on the assignment counts and a p-value of 0.01 on your success metric showing a lift. What do you report?",
    a: "Nothing about the lift. A failing sample ratio check means the two arms are no longer comparable populations, so the effect estimate is not an estimate of the treatment effect.\n\nYou report the SRM, find the mechanism that lost or gained units, fix it and rerun. The lift is not evidence in either direction until then.",
  },
  forecast: { q: "Will the first sample ratio check I run against a live split in my system come back with a p-value above 0.0005?" },
  deepDive: "Help me write the SRM check as a scheduled ClickHouse query over my assignment table, including the chi-squared computation and a sensible alerting threshold."
},
{
  id: "experiments-aa-test",
  track: "experiments", level: "applied",
  title: "Run the experiment against itself first",
  source: "Georgi Georgiev, Statistical Methods in Online A/B Testing, 2019",
  idea: "An A/A test measures the false positive rate of your entire pipeline, not just of your statistical test.",
  why: "In an A/A test both arms get identical treatment, so the true effect is exactly zero and you know the right answer in advance. Under a correctly specified test the p-values across many such comparisons should be uniform on zero to one, which means about five percent land below 0.05 and about one percent below 0.01. That is the only end-to-end check that covers assignment, logging, joins, the variance estimator and the test simultaneously, because it asks the one question you can grade.\n\nWhen the rate comes out high, the cause is nearly always a variance estimate that is too small rather than a broken random number generator. Correlated units counted as independent, sessions analysed as if they were users, an outlier-heavy metric with no capping, or a randomisation that reuses buckets across experiments will all produce standard errors that are honestly computed for the wrong model. The A/A test does not tell you which one, but it tells you that the interval you are about to quote to a VP is narrower than reality.",
  failureMode: "A team ships their first experiment platform, runs twelve experiments, and eight come back significant. Nobody has run an A/A test. When they eventually do, eleven of twenty null comparisons are significant, because the analysis unit is the request and requests from the same user are heavily correlated. Every one of those eight wins is now unfalsifiable.",
  experiment: "Pick a metric you already compute in ClickHouse over a stable past week. Split users into two halves by the parity of a hash of the user id, run your usual comparison, record the p-value. Repeat with twenty different hash seeds. Count how many of the twenty come out below 0.05. One is expected, two is fine, five or more means your variance estimate is wrong and you should look at the analysis unit first.",
  reflection: "How many of your twenty came back significant, and if it was more than three, what is the unit you are treating as independent that is not?",
  recall: {
    q: "An A/A test on your platform returns significant results in 25 percent of runs. What is the most likely cause, and what is the least likely?",
    a: "Most likely: the variance is underestimated, usually because the analysis unit is smaller than the randomisation unit, or because a heavy-tailed metric has no capping. The point estimates are fine; the standard errors are too small.\n\nLeast likely: a broken randomiser. Bad assignment usually shows as sample ratio mismatch or as a consistent directional bias, not as symmetric excess significance.",
  },
  forecast: { q: "Out of twenty A/A comparisons I run this week, will more than three come back significant at the 0.05 level?" },
  deepDive: "Design an A/A test I can run against my existing event tables this afternoon, including how to pick the hash seeds and what to conclude from each possible outcome."
},
{
  id: "experiments-twymans-law",
  track: "experiments", level: "core",
  title: "Any figure that looks interesting is usually wrong",
  source: "Ron Kohavi, Alex Deng and Lukas Vermeer, A/B Testing Intuition Busters: Common Misunderstandings in Online Controlled Experiments, KDD, 2022",
  idea: "A surprisingly large effect is more often an instrumentation bug than a discovery, so the first response to an exciting number is a bug hunt.",
  why: "This is Twyman's law, quoted throughout Kohavi, Tang and Xu, and it is a statement about prior odds rather than about pessimism. Ask how often a single change to a mature product moves a headline metric by twenty percent, and then ask how often a data pipeline has a bug in a given quarter. The second rate is much higher than the first. Conditional on seeing a twenty percent move, the bug hypothesis wins on the prior before you look at the p-value at all.\n\nThe intuition busters paper attacks the reflex that produces the opposite reaction. A small p-value says the data are unlikely under the null, not that the effect is large; effect size and significance are separate readings, and a huge estimate in a small sample is exactly the situation where the estimate is least reliable. Add the winner's curse - the experiments you decide to celebrate are selected on having large observed effects, so their estimates are biased upward - and the surprising result is the one you should trust least, not most.",
  failureMode: "An agent feature shows a forty percent reduction in mean handle time. Everyone is delighted for a week. The cause is that a timeout in the treatment path truncated the long sessions before they logged, so the slowest work simply vanished from the numerator. The metric improved because the failures stopped being measured.",
  experiment: "List the last five numbers in your team's history that made someone say the result was surprising. For each, write down what the eventual explanation was: real effect, instrumentation, population change, or never resolved. Count the reals. If fewer than two of five were real, you now have your own base rate for Twyman's law and can quote it the next time someone wants to ship on a shock result.",
  reflection: "What is the largest metric move you would accept without investigation, and what makes that threshold defensible rather than arbitrary?",
  recall: {
    q: "Two results land: a two percent lift with p equal to 0.01, and a thirty percent lift with p equal to 0.01. Which do you investigate for bugs first, and why?",
    a: "The thirty percent one. The p-values are the same, so significance gives you no way to separate them, but the prior probability of a thirty percent move from a single change is far lower than the prior probability of a pipeline bug.\n\nThe p-value measures compatibility with the null, not plausibility of the estimate. Size of effect is the thing to be sceptical about, and it is judged against your base rate for effects of that size, not against alpha.",
  },
  deepDive: "Here is a surprisingly large result from one of my dashboards; generate the ranked list of instrumentation and population explanations I should rule out before treating it as real."
},
{
  id: "experiments-oec",
  track: "experiments", level: "applied",
  title: "Choosing the overall evaluation criterion is the highest leverage decision in the experiment",
  source: "Alex Deng and Xiaolin Shi, Data-Driven Metric Development for Online Controlled Experiments: Seven Lessons Learned, KDD, 2016",
  idea: "The metric you agree to ship on determines what the organisation will optimise for, so it has to be sensitive, aligned with long term value, and hard to game.",
  why: "Those three properties fight each other. Sensitive metrics are usually proximal: clicks, sessions started, requests served. They move within a two week experiment because they are close to the change and have low variance per user. Aligned metrics are usually distal: retention, renewal, the customer still being here in a year. They are what you actually want and they barely move in two weeks. A metric that fails on sensitivity means every experiment reads flat and the programme stops informing anything; a metric that fails on alignment means you run a fast, rigorous, well-instrumented march in the wrong direction.\n\nDeng and Shi's answer is to treat metric selection as its own empirical problem. Build a corpus of past experiments where you know the verdict, then score candidate metrics on whether they detect the known-good ones and stay flat on the known-null ones. That gives you a measured sensitivity for each candidate instead of an argument in a meeting. The gaming criterion is separate and adversarial: for each candidate, ask what the laziest change is that would move it, and if that change is something you would not want shipped, the metric is not safe as an OEC.",
  failureMode: "An agent team picks number of agent invocations as its success metric because it moves reliably. Within two quarters the product suggests the agent more aggressively in more places, invocations are up thirty percent, and the share of invocations that end in the user accepting the output has quietly halved. Every experiment passed. The product got worse.",
  experiment: "Write down the single metric you would ship your next agent change on. Compute its per-user standard deviation over the last four weeks in ClickHouse, then the minimum detectable effect at your available sample: for eighty percent power at a two-sided five percent level, that is about 2.8 times the standard error of the difference between arms. Compare that to the effect size you actually expect. If the MDE is larger than the expected effect, the metric cannot serve as your OEC and you need a more sensitive one or more traffic. Then write the one-line answer to what is the laziest change that moves this metric.",
  reflection: "Is your candidate OEC failing on sensitivity, on alignment, or on gameability, and which of the three is cheapest for you to fix this quarter?",
  recall: {
    q: "Name the three properties a good OEC needs, and say which two are in direct tension.",
    a: "Sensitivity - it moves detectably within the experiment's duration. Alignment - moving it in the good direction really does correspond to long term value. Resistance to gaming - the cheapest way to move it is not something you would refuse to ship.\n\nSensitivity and alignment are the pair in tension: the metrics closest to the change move most and matter least, while the metrics that matter most move slowly and are swamped by variance.",
  },
  deepDive: "Here are three candidate success metrics for my agent feature; score each on sensitivity, alignment and gameability, and tell me what historical data I would need to settle the disagreement empirically."
},
{
  id: "experiments-guardrails",
  track: "experiments", level: "applied",
  title: "Guardrail metrics exist to catch the harm your success metric cannot see",
  source: "Pavel Dmitriev, Somit Gupta, Dong Woo Kim and Garnet Vaz, A Dirty Dozen: Twelve Common Metric Interpretation Pitfalls in Online Controlled Experiments, KDD, 2017",
  idea: "Every experiment ships with the same fixed set of guardrails regardless of its hypothesis, and a guardrail regression blocks the ship even when the success metric wins.",
  why: "Your success metric is chosen to detect the benefit you hypothesised. It is structurally blind to costs you did not think of, and the costs you did not think of are the reason guardrails are a standing list rather than a per-experiment decision. Latency, error rate, crash rate, abandonment and cost per unit of work belong on every experiment, because the class of change that improves one thing while degrading responsiveness or reliability is enormous and nobody proposes it deliberately.\n\nThe pitfall catalogue adds a second reason, which is that guardrails also detect metrics lying. Several of the dirty dozen are variations on the population under the metric changing: an average over a set whose membership the treatment altered, a per-user rate where treatment changed who counts as a user, a funnel rate improving because the top of the funnel got smaller. Guardrails that track absolute counts alongside the rates make that visible. If your rate improved and your denominator moved by ten percent, you are looking at a composition change and not at an improvement.",
  failureMode: "An agent retrieval change improves answer acceptance rate by four percent and ships. Nobody was watching p99 latency, which went from six seconds to fourteen. Two weeks later usage is down and nobody connects it to the win, because the win was real and the harm was in a metric that was not on the report.",
  experiment: "Write the guardrail list for your agent runtime now, before you need it: p95 and p99 latency, workflow failure rate from Temporal, tool error rate, cost per completed run, and the absolute count of runs. For each, put the current value from Grafana and the regression threshold you would block a ship on. Count how many of the five you can query today without new instrumentation. That count is what your next experiment can actually be protected by.",
  reflection: "Which guardrail on your list has no threshold yet, and is that because the number is hard to get or because you have not decided what harm is acceptable?",
  recall: {
    q: "Your treatment improves task success rate by five percent. What denominator check do you run before believing it, and why?",
    a: "Check whether the number of tasks changed. A rate can improve because the numerator rose or because the denominator lost its hardest cases, and those are opposite findings with the same sign.\n\nReport the absolute counts of numerator and denominator alongside every rate. A rate moving while its denominator also moves is a composition change until proven otherwise.",
  },
  deepDive: "Help me define the five standing guardrails for my agent runtime as concrete Grafana and Temporal queries, with a defensible regression threshold on each."
},
{
  id: "experiments-peeking",
  track: "experiments", level: "core",
  title: "A p-value you watch continuously is not a p-value",
  source: "Ramesh Johari, Pete Koomen, Leonid Pekelis and David Walsh, Peeking at A/B Tests: Why It Matters and What to Do About It, KDD, 2017",
  idea: "A fixed-sample p-value only controls error at the single sample size you committed to in advance, so checking it as data arrives inflates the false positive rate.",
  why: "The guarantee behind a five percent significance level is a statement about one test at one predetermined n. Watching the number as data accumulates turns the test statistic into a random walk and turns your decision into a stopping rule, and a random walk with no drift will wander across any fixed boundary eventually. Armitage, McPherson and Rowe showed in 1969 that testing at 0.05 after each of ten equally spaced looks gives a false positive rate of roughly nineteen percent. Let the looks continue without limit and the probability of eventually crossing goes to one, under the null, with certainty.\n\nThe practical problem is that nobody will stop looking. The dashboard exists, the VP has the link, and the incentive to stop as soon as the result is favourable is exactly what makes the inflation worst, because early crossings are dominated by noise. Johari and colleagues construct an always-valid p-value: a sequence whose running minimum still satisfies the error guarantee, so that the probability that it ever dips below alpha under the null is at most alpha. You give up some power at the planned sample size and buy the freedom to look whenever you like.",
  failureMode: "A two week experiment goes green on day three. It is called early, the feature ships, and the effect never reappears in any later measurement. Nobody committed fraud; the team simply stopped when the walk was on the favourable side of the boundary, which is the single most likely moment for it to be there by chance.",
  experiment: "Simulate it. In a spreadsheet or twenty lines of Python, draw two arms from the same normal distribution, compute the two-sample p-value at ten equally spaced checkpoints, and record whether it ever went below 0.05. Repeat two hundred times and count. You should land near twenty percent rather than five. Then repeat with a single test at the final checkpoint only and confirm you get about five percent.",
  reflection: "Who currently looks at your in-flight experiment numbers, how often, and what would they do if the number went green tomorrow?",
  recall: {
    q: "Explain to a sceptical stakeholder why calling the experiment early on a green day three is not just being decisive.",
    a: "The five percent guarantee applies to one test at a pre-committed sample size. Ten looks at the same threshold gives roughly nineteen percent false positives, and unlimited looking gives certainty of eventually crossing under the null.\n\nStopping when the number first looks good selects precisely the moments where noise is largest relative to signal. If they want to look continuously, the fix is an always-valid p-value or a confidence sequence, not discipline.",
  },
  forecast: { q: "In my own null simulation with ten looks, will the observed crossing rate land between fifteen and twenty-five percent?" },
  deepDive: "Write me the peeking simulation and then show what the same runs look like under an always-valid p-value so I can see the power I am trading away."
},
{
  id: "experiments-anytime-valid",
  track: "experiments", level: "advanced",
  title: "Anytime-valid inference lets you stop whenever you like",
  source: "Aaditya Ramdas, Peter Grunwald, Vladimir Vovk and Glenn Shafer, Game-Theoretic Statistics and Safe Anytime-Valid Inference, Statistical Science, 2023",
  idea: "Evidence accumulated as a betting process gives error guarantees that hold at every stopping time, including stopping times chosen after seeing the data.",
  why: "Recast the test as a game. You bet against the null hypothesis, starting with one unit of wealth, and the null is only allowed to offer you fair bets. Your wealth process is then a non-negative martingale under the null with expectation one at all times. Ville's inequality says the probability that such a process ever reaches 1 over alpha is at most alpha, so reaching twenty means you may reject at the five percent level, no matter when you stop or how many times you looked. An e-value is a snapshot of that wealth: a non-negative statistic whose expectation under the null is at most one, and one over the e-value behaves like a conservative p-value.\n\nFor a Bernoulli outcome this is concrete. Testing whether the success rate is at most p0, pick an alternative p1 above it and multiply your wealth by p1 over p0 on each success and by one minus p1 over one minus p0 on each failure. That product is a test martingale. It has three properties a p-value does not: it composes by multiplication across independent batches, it can be reported mid-flight as a running strength of evidence, and it never punishes you for adding more data.",
  failureMode: "A team runs an agent regression suite nightly and applies a fresh test each morning against the same quality bar. Over a quarter that is ninety tests at five percent, so several alarms are pure noise and the team learns to ignore the alarm. The suite becomes a ritual that has been trained out of anyone's decision loop.",
  experiment: "Take a stream of pass or fail outcomes you already have - Temporal workflow completions, eval results, deploy successes. Set the null at your current known rate p0 and pick p1 as the rate you would care about. Compute the running product in a spreadsheet, one row per outcome, and plot the wealth. If it crosses twenty you have significance at five percent, whenever you choose to stop. If you have no stream yet, simulate two hundred draws at p0 and confirm that the wealth rarely crosses twenty, then simulate at p1 and see how many observations it takes.",
  reflection: "Where in your delivery process do you currently re-test the same hypothesis on a schedule, and what would the wealth process for it look like instead?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is Ville's inequality and what does it buy you over a fixed-sample test?",
    a: "For a non-negative martingale that starts at one, the probability it ever reaches 1 over alpha is at most alpha. So a wealth process built from fair bets against the null can be monitored continuously and rejected whenever it crosses that level.\n\nThe fixed-sample test controls error at one pre-committed sample size only. The martingale controls it at every stopping time, which is what you need when someone will read the dashboard every morning regardless of what you asked them to do.",
  },
  deepDive: "Help me build a betting martingale for my agent eval pass rate, including how to choose the alternative rate and what wealth threshold to alert on."
},
{
  id: "experiments-false-discovery",
  track: "experiments", level: "applied",
  title: "Twenty metrics means one significant result by chance",
  source: "Yoav Benjamini and Yosef Hochberg, Controlling the False Discovery Rate, Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B, 1995",
  idea: "When you test many metrics or many segments at once, control the expected proportion of false findings among your findings rather than the error rate of each test.",
  why: "With twenty independent null comparisons at a five percent level, the expected number of false positives is one, and the probability of at least one is 1 minus 0.95 to the twentieth, which is 0.64. That is not a subtle effect and it is the default state of any experiment readout that reports thirty metrics. Bonferroni fixes it by dividing alpha by the number of tests, but that controls the probability of any false positive at all, which is a stricter target than you need when you are scanning and expect several real effects.\n\nBenjamini and Hochberg target the false discovery rate instead: the expected fraction of your rejections that are wrong. The procedure is a sort and a threshold. Order the p-values ascending as p(1) through p(m), find the largest k for which p(k) is at most k over m times q, and reject everything up to and including that k. Set q at 0.1 and you are saying you will tolerate one in ten of your declared findings being noise. It is far more powerful than Bonferroni when many effects are real, and it degrades gracefully as the number of tests grows.",
  failureMode: "An experiment readout shows twenty-eight metrics. Three are significant, two of them in directions nobody predicted, and the writeup builds a story around the two surprising ones. Under BH at q equals 0.1, none of the three survives. The story was constructed from the tail of a null distribution.",
  experiment: "Take the last multi-metric comparison you looked at, in Grafana or a spreadsheet, and count m, the number of comparisons that were actually on the page including the segment breakdowns nobody mentioned. Compute 1 minus 0.95 to the power m as the probability of at least one spurious hit. Then apply BH at q equals 0.1 to the p-values and count survivors. If you cannot get p-values, generate twenty uniform random numbers as synthetic p-values and run the procedure on those so the mechanics are familiar.",
  reflection: "How many metrics are on your standard experiment readout, and how many of them are genuinely part of the decision rather than context?",
  recall: {
    q: "State the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure, and say what it controls that Bonferroni does not.",
    a: "Sort the m p-values ascending. Find the largest k such that p(k) is at most k times q over m. Reject hypotheses one through k.\n\nBH controls the expected proportion of false positives among the rejections at level q. Bonferroni controls the probability of making even one false rejection, which is a stricter goal and costs a lot of power when several effects are genuinely real.",
  },
  deepDive: "Here is the metric list from my experiment readout; tell me which of them are decision metrics and how to apply BH to the rest without losing the signal I care about."
},
{
  id: "experiments-ratio-metrics",
  track: "experiments", level: "advanced",
  title: "A ratio of user-level quantities needs the delta method, not a t-test on rows",
  source: "Alex Deng, Ulf Knoblich and Jiannan Lu, Applying the Delta Method in Metric Analytics: A Practical Guide with Novel Ideas, KDD, 2018",
  idea: "When the randomisation unit is the user and the analysis unit is the session or the request, the variance of the ratio has to come from the joint variance of numerator and denominator at user level.",
  why: "Take a metric like tool calls per session. The natural computation is total tool calls divided by total sessions, and the natural test is a t-test over rows. That test assumes the rows are independent, and they are not: sessions from the same user are correlated, and users were the thing you randomised. The result is a standard error that can be several times too small, which is exactly the pathology an A/A test surfaces as excess significance.\n\nThe fix is to aggregate to the randomisation unit first, then apply the delta method to the ratio of the two means. Let X be per-user numerator and Y be per-user denominator, with R equal to mean X over mean Y. Then the variance of R is approximately one over n, times one over mean Y squared, times the quantity variance of X minus two R times covariance of X and Y plus R squared times variance of Y. The covariance term is what the naive computation throws away, and its sign matters: heavy users contribute large values to both numerator and denominator, so ignoring the correlation usually inflates the apparent precision.",
  failureMode: "Acceptance rate per agent invocation is analysed at the invocation level. A handful of power users generate forty percent of the invocations, so the effective sample size is nowhere near the row count. The confidence interval is quoted as plus or minus half a percent, the true interval is plus or minus two percent, and the team ships on a difference that is inside the noise.",
  experiment: "Pick a ratio metric you report. In ClickHouse compute, per user, the numerator sum X and the denominator count Y. Then compute mean X, mean Y, variance of X, variance of Y and the covariance, and assemble the delta-method standard error from the formula above. Compare it against the standard error you get from a naive test over raw rows. Report the ratio of the two. If it is above 1.5, every interval you have quoted on that metric has been too narrow.",
  reflection: "Which of your reported metrics have a denominator that the treatment itself can change, and what does that do to the interpretation on top of the variance problem?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why can you not simply run a t-test over sessions for a per-session metric in a user-randomised experiment?",
    a: "Sessions from the same user are correlated, so treating them as independent observations understates the variance. The effective sample size is closer to the number of users than the number of sessions.\n\nAggregate to the user, then apply the delta method to the ratio of means, which accounts for the variance of numerator, the variance of denominator and the covariance between them.",
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my ratio metric and its table schema; write the ClickHouse query that produces the delta-method standard error and shows me how far off the naive one is."
},
{
  id: "experiments-novelty-primacy",
  track: "experiments", level: "applied",
  title: "The first week of a new feature measures novelty, not value",
  source: "Ron Kohavi, Roger Longbotham, Dan Sommerfield and Randal M. Henne, Controlled Experiments on the Web: Survey and Practical Guide, Data Mining and Knowledge Discovery, 2009",
  idea: "Treatment effects that change systematically with time since first exposure mean the pooled estimate is not an estimate of anything durable.",
  why: "Two opposite transients contaminate short experiments. Novelty: users click the new thing because it is new, the effect is large in the first days and decays towards nothing or towards negative as the novelty wears off. Primacy: experienced users are slower with a changed interface or a changed workflow, so the effect starts negative and recovers as they relearn. Both are real user behaviour, both are temporary, and both are invisible in a single pooled number averaged over the experiment window.\n\nThe diagnostic is not a longer experiment, it is a different plot. Cohort users by the day they were first exposed, then plot the treatment effect against days since first exposure rather than against calendar date. Calendar date confounds the transient with the ramp of new users entering the experiment. If the by-exposure-day curve is flat, the pooled number means something. If it is monotonically decaying, your pooled number is an average over a decay curve, and its value depends on how long you happened to run.",
  failureMode: "An agent gets a new suggestion surface. Week one shows a twenty-two percent lift in engagement, the launch is approved, and by week six engagement is one percent below control. The pooled two week number was a weighted average of a novelty spike and the real effect, dominated by the spike because most users were newly exposed during the window.",
  experiment: "Take any feature you launched in the last quarter where you have per-user event data. In ClickHouse, assign each user a cohort by their first-exposure date, compute your key metric per cohort per day-since-exposure, and plot the curve. You do not need a control arm for the diagnostic version: you are looking at whether the metric for a cohort is stable from day one to day fourteen. A curve that halves over two weeks tells you what your first week readings have been worth.",
  reflection: "Does the by-exposure-day curve for your last launch flatten, and if so at what day - is that day inside or outside your usual experiment duration?",
  recall: {
    q: "You suspect novelty. Why is plotting the treatment effect by calendar date not enough?",
    a: "Calendar date mixes the transient with the composition of the experiment population. Users enter the experiment on different days, so on any given date you are averaging over people at different stages of exposure.\n\nCohort by first-exposure date and plot against days since exposure. That separates how long the user has had the feature from when in the experiment you are looking.",
  },
  deepDive: "Write the ClickHouse query that cohorts my users by first exposure and plots my metric against days since exposure, and tell me what shapes would mean novelty versus primacy versus a real stable effect."
},
{
  id: "experiments-long-term-holdback",
  track: "experiments", level: "advanced",
  title: "Long term effects need a holdback, because short term metrics can move the wrong way",
  source: "Henning Hohnhold, Deirdre O'Brien and Diane Tang, Focusing on the Long-term: It's Good for Users and Business, KDD, 2015",
  idea: "Users learn, and the learned response can reverse the sign of the short term measurement, so some experiments have to run for months against a held-back population.",
  why: "Google's ads work is the cleanest published demonstration. Increasing ad load raises revenue immediately and measurably. Over months, users learn to ignore ads, click-through decays, and the long run revenue effect is far worse than the short run reading implied. Hohnhold and colleagues separated the immediate effect from the learned effect using long running experiments and a randomisation scheme that re-randomises a fraction daily, which lets you compare users who have lived under the treatment for months against users who only just arrived in it. The learned effect turned out to be large enough that Google reduced ad load on mobile search on the strength of it.\n\nThe generalisable point is that any feature which trains a habit, a trust level or an ignoring reflex has a learned component the two week experiment cannot see. For an agent this is direct: if the agent is confidently wrong ten percent of the time, the immediate metric is task completion and the learned metric is whether people stop reading its output carefully, or stop invoking it at all. The learned effect arrives after the experiment has been declared a win and the arm has been turned off.",
  failureMode: "An agent is tuned to always produce an answer rather than say it cannot help. Task completion rises, the experiment ships, the holdback is retired. Six months later invocations are flat and the qualitative feedback says people no longer trust the output. There is no arm left to measure against, so the reversal is deniable and nobody can attribute it.",
  experiment: "Pick the last significant thing your team shipped and check whether any population is still on the old behaviour. If not, write the holdback plan for the next launch into the Jira ticket before it starts: what percentage stays on control, which two metrics get read, and the calendar date of the read. One percent held back for ninety days costs almost nothing and is the only way you will ever answer the question.",
  reflection: "Which of your features would you expect to have a learned effect with the opposite sign to its launch metric, and what would that learned metric be?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is a learned effect, and why does a standard two week experiment systematically miss it?",
    a: "A learned effect is the change in user behaviour that accumulates from repeated exposure, such as learning to ignore ads or learning not to trust an agent's output. It builds over weeks to months.\n\nA two week experiment measures users who are all recently exposed, so it estimates the immediate effect only. Detecting the learned component needs a long-running holdback, or a design that compares long-exposed users against freshly randomised ones.",
  },
  deepDive: "Help me design a ninety day holdback for my next agent launch: what fraction to hold, which metrics to read, and how to keep the arm alive through the usual pressure to retire it."
},
{
  id: "experiments-interference",
  track: "experiments", level: "advanced",
  title: "When units affect each other, unit-level randomisation measures the wrong thing",
  source: "Johan Ugander, Brian Karrer, Lars Backstrom and Jon Kleinberg, Graph Cluster Randomization: Network Exposure to Multiple Universes, KDD, 2013",
  idea: "If a treated user changes the experience of a control user, the difference between arms is not the treatment effect, and the fix is to randomise clusters of connected units.",
  why: "Unit-level randomisation relies on the assumption that a unit's outcome depends only on its own assignment. Interference breaks it in both directions. Positive spillover, where treated users make the product better for their untreated colleagues, contaminates control, shrinks the measured gap and biases the estimate towards zero. Competitive spillover, where treated users consume a shared resource such as queue capacity or rate-limited model tokens, degrades control and inflates the measured gap. Same design, opposite bias, and you cannot tell which you have from the readout.\n\nIn a data catalog this is not exotic. Users in the same workspace read each other's annotations, lineage and documentation, so a treated user who documents more assets changes the experience of every untreated user in that tenant. Ugander and colleagues randomise clusters chosen so that most of a unit's neighbours share its assignment, which restores a well defined contrast between a mostly-treated and a mostly-untreated world. The cost is power: your effective sample size is the number of clusters, and the variance inflation is roughly one plus the average cluster size minus one, times the intra-cluster correlation, the design effect Leslie Kish set out in Survey Sampling in 1965.",
  failureMode: "An experiment on automatic asset descriptions is randomised by user. Treated users generate descriptions that everyone in the tenant can see, including control users. The measured lift is small, the feature is judged marginal and deprioritised. The real effect was substantial; it just leaked into control through the shared workspace.",
  experiment: "Run the contamination check. Assign every active user to a synthetic arm by hash, then in ClickHouse count what fraction of workspaces contain users from both arms, and what fraction of your active users sit in such a mixed workspace. If it is most of them, unit-level randomisation on any feature with shared visibility is measuring a diluted contrast. Then compute what tenant-level randomisation would cost you: count the tenants, and apply the design effect using an estimate of intra-cluster correlation from the between-tenant variance you already have.",
  reflection: "Which of your features produce artefacts other users can see, and for those, is your unit of randomisation the user or the tenant?",
  recall: {
    q: "How do you tell whether interference biases your estimate towards zero or away from it?",
    a: "By the sign of the spillover, not by the data. Positive spillover improves control and shrinks the measured difference towards zero. Competition for a shared resource degrades control and inflates the measured difference.\n\nThe readout looks the same either way, so you have to reason about the mechanism. If treated and control units share visibility, capacity or a market, unit randomisation is not estimating the thing you want.",
  },
  deepDive: "My users share workspaces and see each other's outputs; help me decide between tenant-level randomisation and a graph cluster design, and quantify the power I would lose."
},
{
  id: "experiments-switchback",
  track: "experiments", level: "advanced",
  title: "Switch the whole system back and forth when you cannot split the population",
  source: "Iavor Bojinov, David Simchi-Levi and Jinglong Zhao, Design and Analysis of Switchback Experiments, Management Science, 2023",
  idea: "When every unit contends for the same shared resource, randomise over time instead of over units by alternating the whole system between treatment and control.",
  why: "A switchback puts the entire system in one condition for an interval, then switches, and estimates the effect from the difference between intervals. It sidesteps interference completely, because within any interval there is no other arm to leak into. That makes it the right design for anything with a shared bottleneck: a model endpoint with finite throughput, a worker pool, a global rate limit, a matching market.\n\nThe whole design reduces to one parameter, the switching interval, and it trades off two things. Short intervals give you more switches and therefore more effective observations, but the carryover from the previous condition contaminates the start of each interval, biasing the estimate towards no difference. Long intervals are clean but give you very few independent observations, so the variance is large and the estimate is exposed to time-of-day and day-of-week structure. Bojinov and colleagues formalise this under an assumption of carryover of bounded order and show that regularly spaced switches with a randomised starting point minimise mean squared error. The engineering job is to measure the carryover length rather than guess it.",
  failureMode: "A team switchbacks a routing change on an hourly cadence. The cache warms for twenty minutes after every switch, so a third of every interval is measuring the previous condition. The estimate is dragged towards zero, the change reads as no effect, and the real regression ships because the design could not see it.",
  experiment: "Measure your carryover length from data you already have. Find a past config change in Grafana and look at how long your key metric took to settle to its new level after the deploy: cache warm, queue drain, autoscaler response. That settling time is the minimum interval you can use, and the interval must be several times longer than it. Then compute your effective observation count: experiment duration divided by interval, minus the discarded warm-up in each. If that number is under about twenty you do not have a workable switchback and need a longer run.",
  reflection: "What is the settling time of your system after a config change, and does it allow enough switches inside a week to be worth running?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is the central design parameter of a switchback experiment, and what does it trade off?",
    a: "The switching interval. Shorter intervals give more switches and more effective observations, but a larger fraction of each interval is contaminated by carryover from the previous condition, which biases the effect towards zero.\n\nLonger intervals are clean but leave few independent observations and expose the estimate to time-of-day structure. Measure the settling time of the system empirically and set the interval to a multiple of it.",
  },
  deepDive: "My agents share a rate-limited model endpoint so user-level splits are contaminated; help me design a switchback, starting from how to measure the carryover length in Grafana."
},
{
  id: "experiments-heterogeneity",
  track: "experiments", level: "advanced",
  title: "Segment-level effects are mostly noise until you estimate them properly",
  source: "Stefan Wager and Susan Athey, Estimation and Inference of Heterogeneous Treatment Effects Using Random Forests, Journal of the American Statistical Association, 2018",
  idea: "Slicing a flat experiment until some segment turns significant is a search procedure, and its output needs either prespecification or an estimator built for the search.",
  why: "The mechanics are the multiple comparisons problem wearing different clothes. Ten segment dimensions crossed with five metrics is fifty comparisons, and at a five percent level you expect two or three hits from noise alone. Because the search stops when something interesting appears, the surviving segment is selected precisely on having an extreme estimate, so its effect size is biased upward as well as being probably spurious. The story that gets written afterwards - enterprise tenants respond differently because of their governance workflows - is generated after the fact and is unfalsifiable within the same data.\n\nCausal forests attack this by making the search part of the estimator and then paying for it honestly. The forest splits on covariates to find regions where the treatment effect differs, and honesty means each tree uses one subsample to decide the splits and a disjoint subsample to estimate the effect inside the resulting leaves. That separation removes the bias that comes from choosing a split because the data in it looked extreme, and Wager and Athey prove the resulting estimates are asymptotically normal with a computable variance, so you get pointwise confidence intervals on heterogeneity you did not prespecify.",
  failureMode: "An experiment reads flat overall. Someone slices by plan tier, region, tenant size and connector type until enterprise customers in EMEA show a nine percent lift with p equal to 0.03. That segment becomes the launch justification and a roadmap commitment. The next quarter's data shows nothing there, and by then two engineers have spent a quarter on it.",
  experiment: "Reconstruct the search on a past result. Count the segment dimensions and metrics on the readout, multiply to get k comparisons, and compute 1 minus 0.95 to the power k as the chance of at least one spurious hit. Then do the prospective half: for your next experiment, write into the Jira ticket, before launch, the at most two segments you will look at and the direction you expect. Anything found outside that list becomes a hypothesis for a new experiment rather than a finding in this one.",
  reflection: "Which segment story does your team currently believe, and was it prespecified or discovered in a slice?",
  recall: {
    q: "What does honesty mean in a causal forest, and what problem does it solve?",
    a: "Each tree uses one subsample of the data to choose its splits and a separate, disjoint subsample to estimate the treatment effect within the resulting leaves.\n\nWithout that separation, a leaf is selected because its data looked extreme and then the same data is used to estimate the effect, which biases the estimate away from zero. Honesty makes the leaf estimates asymptotically normal, which is what allows valid confidence intervals on effects you did not prespecify.",
  },
  deepDive: "Here is a segment finding from a flat experiment; help me work out how many comparisons the search really covered and what a prespecified follow-up would need to look like."
},
{
  id: "experiments-most-show-nothing",
  track: "experiments", level: "core",
  title: "Most experiments show nothing, and that is the finding",
  source: "Ron Kohavi, Thomas Crook and Roger Longbotham, Online Experimentation at Microsoft, Third Workshop on Data Mining Case Studies, 2009",
  idea: "Only about a third of well designed ideas improve the metric they were built to improve, so a programme that rarely reports a null is reporting false positives.",
  why: "The Microsoft case studies paper puts it plainly: roughly one third of ideas improved the metrics they targeted, one third made no difference, and one third made things worse. These were ideas that survived design review and got built, proposed by people who knew the product. The number is not an indictment of anyone's judgement; it is what the base rate for product intuition actually looks like when you measure it instead of assuming it.\n\nThat base rate is the yardstick for your own programme. If nine of your last ten experiments were declared wins, the likely explanations are not that your team is three times better than Bing's. They are that success metrics are being chosen after the readout, that nulls are quietly not written up, that the analysis stops at the first favourable slice, or that the changes are so large and obvious that a controlled experiment was never the constraint. A healthy programme produces a lot of nulls, and the nulls are the return on the investment: they are the quarters you did not spend building on a false premise.",
  failureMode: "Every launch retro in the last year concludes that the feature performed well. No document anywhere records a change that did nothing. The organisation has learned that reporting a null is career-negative, so the experiment programme now functions as a ratification step and the base rate of one in three is being paid in engineering time nobody is counting.",
  experiment: "Open your Jira board and take the last ten shipped features. For each, record two things: was there a success metric written down before the work started, and was it measured after ship. Count how many have both. Then count how many were publicly described as successes. Compare the declared success rate against one in three. If you have far fewer than ten with pre-declared metrics, that number is the finding and the success rate is not interpretable.",
  reflection: "What happens socially in your team when an experiment comes back null, and what would have to change for that outcome to be reported as readily as a win?",
  recall: {
    q: "Your team reports that eight of its last ten changes improved their target metric. Why is that a warning sign rather than good news?",
    a: "The published base rate from Microsoft is that about one third of well designed ideas improve their target metric, one third do nothing and one third hurt. Eight in ten is far outside that.\n\nThe usual explanations are metrics chosen after seeing the data, nulls not being written up, or analysis that stops at the first favourable slice. A programme with no nulls is not detecting effects, it is confirming intentions.",
  },
  forecast: { q: "When I review my last ten shipped features, will fewer than three of them have had a success metric written down before the work started?" },
  deepDive: "Help me audit my last ten shipped features against pre-declared metrics and work out what my team's real hit rate has been."
}
);
